                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_constraint_right: `<strong>That is the right diagnosis.</strong> The binding constraint is readiness — AeroFoam-7 is TRL 4, buyers buy at TRL 7, and the €450k field-validation gap is the valley of death. Now you can fund the crossing instead of pretending lab data sells itself.`,
                    coach_r1_constraint_none: `<strong>Readiness denial — the most expensive mistake here.</strong> Lab data is not a sale. The construction group will ask for a field demonstration in an operational building, and you are at TRL 4. You cannot close that sale across a gap you refuse to name.`,
                    coach_r1_constraint_time: `<strong>The patent clock is real, but it is not the binding constraint.</strong> Even with the patent filed, a TRL-4 material with no field data does not sell. Time pressures the IP decision; readiness blocks the market.`,
                    coach_r1_constraint_cash: `<strong>Cash is tight, but framing it as the constraint misses the lever.</strong> The seed fund is small precisely because it is meant to mobilise external co-funding. The deeper gap is readiness — and a partner can co-fund it.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named readiness as the binding constraint AND identified the field demonstration TRL 7 actually requires. That clarity is what lets you size the €450k validation gap and design a pathway that funds the crossing.`,
                    coach_r1_paper:         `<strong>A paper is not field validation.</strong> Publishing the lab data is good for academic credit and worthless for a TRL-7 buyer who needs a prototype demonstrated in an operational environment. You are confusing publication with readiness.`,
                    coach_r1_lab:           `<strong>More bench tests just re-prove what you already know.</strong> The bench data already passed — that is why you are at TRL 4, not TRL 2. TRL 7 needs the material proven in the field, not another lab run.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>This is how deep tech dies in the valley.</strong> Low honesty about the readiness gap means you will pitch buyers on lab hype and lose them at the field-demo question. Rate the gap honestly or you will commit money against a fantasy.`,
                    coach_r2_jda:           `<strong>The joint-development agreement fits this context.</strong> A partner co-funds the €450k validation in exchange for a licensing option — sharing the risk of a TRL-4 asset and the cost of crossing the valley, without betting the venture on a reluctant founder. That is matching pathway to context.`,
                    coach_r2_spinoff:       `<strong>You have chosen the route with the biggest payoff on paper.</strong> But your lead inventor has never run a company and is not sure she will leave the university — and the VC's €1.2M is conditional on exactly that. If she stays, who runs your spin-off, and what is a founderless deep-tech company worth?`,
                    coach_r2_license:       `<strong>A bare exclusive license is fast clean cash — and a surrender of control.</strong> You would hand an incumbent exclusive rights to a recyclable material that could reshape the category, and they make their margins on the old product. What happens if they shelve it to protect their line?`,
                    coach_r2_founderless:   `<strong>You are forcing a reluctant scientist into the CEO chair to chase the spin-off upside.</strong> If she walks — and she might — the VC's €1.2M evaporates and you are left with a founderless deep-tech company. Match the pathway to the team you actually have, not the one you wish you had.`,
                    coach_r2_forceceo:      `<strong>Betting on the inventor becoming CEO is the key-person risk made worse.</strong> She has never run a company and is ambivalent about leaving academia. Keep her as the scientific advisor and do not make the whole pathway depend on a role she does not want.`,
                    coach_r2_signing:       `<strong>Handing over rights at signing gives away your leverage early.</strong> Before the field data exists, the asset is at its most uncertain and least valuable. Transfer value to the partner at validated milestones, not at the signature.`,
                    coach_r2_never:         `<strong>No milestones means no structure.</strong> "Keep everything in-house and figure it out later" is how the seed fund gets clawed back and the patent lapses. Define the milestones at which value and risk move to the partner.`,
                    coach_r3_focused:       `<strong>Focused filing is disciplined IP strategy.</strong> Protecting the 2–3 markets of AeroFoam-7's first customers defends the value that matters and keeps budget free for validation. You can let the speculative jurisdictions wait until the material is field-proven.`,
                    coach_r3_underfiled:    `<strong>You under-funded even the focused filing.</strong> National phase has hard costs; a half-funded filing risks lapsing in the markets you actually need. Fund the core jurisdictions properly.`,
                    coach_r3_overfile:      `<strong>Filing in eight jurisdictions to be safe burns the budget.</strong> That is most of your patent money on markets you may never serve, before you even know the material works in the field. Which two or three jurisdictions actually matter — and could the rest wait?`,
                    coach_r3_overfile_short: `<strong>You chose to file everywhere but did not fund it.</strong> Eight jurisdictions cost far more than you allocated, so you protect nothing properly and starve validation too. Focus the filing.`,
                    coach_r3_abandon:       `<strong>Abandoning the patent throws away your deal leverage.</strong> A recyclable aerogel with no IP position is far harder to license or spin out, and the national-phase window does not reopen. Trade secret will not protect a material you have to demonstrate to buyers.`,
                    coach_r3_multinational: `<strong>€250k upfront and a 3% royalty — fast, low-risk, done.</strong> But you have handed an incumbent exclusive control of a recyclable material that could reshape the category, and they make their margins on the old product. What did you trade for the easy cash?`,
                    coach_r3_vc_conditional: `<strong>The VC's €1.2M is conditional on the inventor leaving academia.</strong> Without a willing founder that money is not really on the table — counting it as leverage is counting money you cannot draw. The conditional capital evaporates with the founder.`,
                    coach_r3_noleverage:    `<strong>You spent the whole seed fund internally and pulled in zero external euros.</strong> You may have protected the IP and advanced readiness — but your funder measures you on leverage. Could a partner have co-funded that €450k validation, leaving your seed fund to do something only it can do?`,
                    coach_r3_nopartner:     `<strong>No partner means no co-funding and no leverage.</strong> The whole point of a small seed fund is to mobilise larger external capital. Signing no one leaves you to carry the €450k valley of death alone.`,
                    coach_r4_deadline:      `<strong>Deadline-driven sequencing is exactly right.</strong> Filing in week 1, starting validation immediately and gating partner payments to milestones respects the two irreversible clocks. What is irreversible if you miss week 1 — and you have made sure you will not.`,
                    coach_r4_validatefirst: `<strong>Validating before filing risks the patent.</strong> The national-phase deadline is hard at Day 90; if you wait for validation data the window can close and you lose protection in key markets. File first, then validate.`,
                    coach_r4_loose:         `<strong>Loose sequencing loses the patent and the cash.</strong> "File in the coming months" and "start validation soon" collide with a hard Day-90 deadline and a seed fund that claws back unspent money the same quarter. What happens in week 1?`,
                    coach_r4_kpivalue:      `<strong>A headline number is not a plan.</strong> The biggest deal value may impress for a minute, but the committee funds offices on leverage and readiness delivered. Commit to TRL at exit, time-to-market, value AND the leverage ratio.`,
                    coach_r4_kpinone:       `<strong>No hard KPIs means no accountability.</strong> "Report progress informally" is how the next dormant spin-off begins. Name the metrics the committee can hold you to.`,
                    coach_r4_nocontingency: `<strong>No contingency is a brittle plan.</strong> The inventor can still walk and the field validation can still come back weak in month nine. A committee-ready plan names what is reversible and what is sunk if either happens.`,
                    coach_r4_robust:        `<strong>That is a committee-ready, robust plan.</strong> Deadline-driven sequencing, the full KPI set including leverage, and a contingency for both inventor exit and a validation slip — you have built a pathway that survives if the science or the founder disappoints.`,

